---
import CaseStudyLayout from "@layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import { caseStudies } from "@data/case-studies";
const meta = caseStudies.find((s) => s.id === "quantalyric-mvp")!;
import SectionHeading from "@components/SectionHeading.astro";
import Description from "@components/Description.astro";
import CaseStudyStatCard from "@components/CaseStudyStatCard.astro";
import CaseStudyDecisionCard from "@components/CaseStudyDecisionCard.astro";
import TextAndImageBlock from "@components/TextAndImageBlock.astro";
import QuantaLyricUX from "@img/quantalyric-user-research.png";
---

<CaseStudyLayout {...meta}>
	<section>
		<SectionHeading title="Constraints, but not barriers." number={2} />
		<Description
			fontFamily="var(--text-font-body)"
			size="var(--text-base)"
			color="var(--color-black-umber)"
			margin="var(--space-4) var(--space-0) var(--space-16)"
		>
			I had to translate this grand vision into a realistic and achievable goal.
			They are a pre-seed startup with limited people, time, and
			resources.Together we honed in on the one big idea that gets them funded:
			state of the art AI/ML methodologies that produce more accurate
			forecasting models.
		</Description>
		<div class="stat-cards">
			<CaseStudyStatCard value="90" label="Hours" />
			<CaseStudyStatCard value="5" label="Projects" />
			<CaseStudyStatCard value="1" label="Designer" />
			<CaseStudyStatCard value="1 → 2" label="Developers" />
			<CaseStudyStatCard value="4" label="Design Disciplines" />
		</div>
		<CaseStudyDecisionCard
			title="Cut the MVP from two audiences to one."
			description="The original MVP served energy traders <strong>and</strong> ML engineers with both business intelligence and operational tooling. I pushed to ship forecasting for traders first — and design the rest, but not build it yet."
			nonDecision="Cover both audiences"
			theDecision="Ship a sharp investor demo"
			outcome="Scope cut roughly in half"
			internalSignals={[
				[
					"Team capacity",
					"one part-time developer at the beginning and expanded to two developers for the last project — building two parallel product surfaces was not realistic.",
				],
				[
					"Founder energy",
					"in workshops was highest when describing the forecasting accuracy story; MLOps tooling was talked about as “table stakes.”",
				],
				[
					"One-sentence value prop",
					"the forecasting model was the only thing I could explain in one sentence — that’s the thing investors would remember.",
				],
			]}
			externalSignals={[
				[
					"User pain",
					"traders had dollar-attached losses every week; ML engineers had workarounds.",
				],
				[
					"Competitive gap",
					"competitor MLOps was mature; competitor forecasting accuracy claims were vague. That gap was the wedge.",
				],
				[
					"Pitch feedback",
					"consistently asked “what’s the moat” — accuracy demos answered it; admin tooling didn’t.",
				],
			]}
			fullOutcome="Scope cut roughly in half. The full product was still designed end-to-end so the team could communicate vision, but build priority focused on the trader-facing forecast surface."
		/>
		<TextAndImageBlock
			browline="Scope control."
			image={QuantaLyricUX}
			imageAlt="Quantalyric UX research."
		>
			<p>
				An MVE is the simplest version of a product that's ready for real users;
				it covers every surface they'd need. An MVP can be narrower: a technical
				demo built for one audience. QuantaLyric needed both, but not both
				built. I designed the MVE so the team could communicate the full vision,
				and built the MVP: the reporting surface, as the technical demo for
				investors.
			</p>
			<p>
				From my research, the AI/ML lifecycle has four phases: model
				experimentation, production development, reporting, and CI/CD. Reporting
				is where QuantaLyric's value proposition is fully realized, so that's
				the phase we prioritized for funding.
			</p>
		</TextAndImageBlock>
		<TextAndImageBlock
			browline="The supporting ecosystem of the MVE."
			image={QuantaLyricUX}
			imageAlt="Quantalyric UX research."
		>
			<p>
				ML engineers and data scientists are our secondary personas, and their
				surfaces are essential to the MVE. These are surfaces that explain
				whether a model is working correctly or not. As an example, this mock up
				shows logs and metadata for processing the data from a model that will
				be used to create a forecast report. Our secondary personas can take
				this information and use it to fix faulty models in their preferred
				tools (like Weights and Biases and Jupyter Notebooks).
			</p>
		</TextAndImageBlock>
	</section>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
	section {
		padding: var(--space-20) 0;
	}
	.stat-cards {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);
		gap: var(--space-2);
		justify-content: space-between;
		padding: var(--space-0) var(--space-10);
	}
</style>
